    1	# MPS / Tensor-Train Bond Dimension of the Prime Indicator
    2	
    3	**Script:** `mps_bond_dimension.py`
    4	**Date:** 2026-04-25 (extended same day with base-W sweep)
    5	**Verdict:** CLOSED -- bond dimension of chi_P viewed in base W is exactly
    6	`min(W^j, phi(W) * W^{d-j-1} + 1)` at every cut j, for every (W, d) tested.
    7	Half-cut bond dim ratio chi_P / random asymptotes to `phi(W) / W`. No
    8	polylog MPS representation exists.
    9	
    10	## What was tested
    11	
    12	We view the binary prime indicator `chi_P(n)` for `n in [1, W^d]` as a
    13	state vector in `(C^W)^{otimes d}` and measure its tensor-train bond
    14	dimension at every bipartite cut. Two families:
    15	
    16	  - Family A: `W = 2`, `d in {10, 12, 14, 16, 18, 20}` -- replicates the
    17	    original measurement; also tracks random control, cumulative pi(n),
    18	    and the residual `delta(n) = pi(n) - li(n)`.
    19	  - Family B: `W in {2, 6, 30, 210}` (primorials), various d, exact rank
    20	    at every cut. Tests the precise prediction
    21	    `rank(cut j) = min(W^j, phi(W) * W^{d-j-1} + 1)`.
    22	
    23	A polylog MPS representation would mean polylog-time exact computation of
    24	`pi(N)` and `p(n)`. Compared against a random binary vector at matched
    25	density.
    26	
    27	This builds on but is distinct from earlier project work:
    28	  - `tensor_sieve.py` -- product DFA states, primorial blowup, different object
    29	  - `sieve_matrix_rank.py` -- divisibility matrix `M[n, p]`, different tensor
    30	  - `wavelet_*` -- single-mode basis sparsity, not multipartite
