    Based on the search results, here are the key findings related to the Maynard-Tao sieve, weights computation, and primes:
    
    ## Overview of the Maynard-Tao Sieve
    
    Maynard and Tao independently introduced a variant of the GPY method, relying on multi-dimensional sieve weights, and showed that for any m, there exist infinitely many intervals containing m primes within bounded length.
    
    ## Sieve Weights and Computation
    
    The approach uses weights mimicking "Selberg sieve" weights, with standard choice λd = µ(d)(log R/d)^k, finding S ≈θ if k is large enough. To overcome certain technical challenges, one groups the shifts into various clusters chosen in such a way that the probability that at least one is prime is bounded uniformly from below.
    
    ## Recent Developments (2025-2026)
    
    Recent research continues to apply and extend these methods. A 2025 preprint uses the GPY/Maynard approach with multi-dimensional sieve weights to study bounded gaps between primes. Additionally, the Maynard-Tao Theorem states that for an admissible tuple H containing at least km elements, there are infinitely many n such that n+h is prime for at least m elements h in H.
    
    ## Applications to Prime Gaps
    
    Advancements made by Maynard and Polymath8b have given tools to study prime gaps with improved bounds. These tools allow researchers to study bounds related to smooth prime gaps and show there are infinitely many m-tuples of primes whose gaps are all y_m-smooth for a calculable prime y_m.
    
    
