    ## Recent Research on Prime Counting Function Algorithms
    
    The prime-counting function π(x) computes the number of primes smaller or equal to a given real number and has long-standing interest in number theory, with one method proposed to compute π(x) with time complexity O(x^1/2) without requiring the non-trivial zeros of the Riemann zeta function.
    
    Several approaches to computing π(x) have been developed:
    
    1. **Analytic Methods**: A rigorous implementation of the Lagarias and Odlyzko Analytic Method has been used to evaluate the prime counting function and compute the number of primes less than 10^24.
    
    2. **Combinatorial Approaches**: Recent advances in the combinatorial method for computing π(x) have reduced memory usage by a factor of log x and incorporated modifications for shared- and distributed-memory parallelism, with the method computing π(x) with complexity O(x^2/3 log^-2 x) in time and O(x^1/3 log^2 x) in space.
    
    3. **Elementary Closed-Form Expressions**: Recent work has presented the first fixed-length elementary closed-form expressions for the prime-counting function π(n) and the n-th prime number, using only a finite number of elementary arithmetic operations.
    
    These results demonstrate ongoing progress in developing faster and more efficient algorithms for computing the prime counting function across different mathematical approaches.
    
    
