    Based on the search results, here's what I found about Ken Ono's work on MacMahon partition functions and prime detection:
    
    ## Overview
    
    Integer partitions detect prime numbers in an unexpected way. The primes are the solutions to special equations in partition functions.
    
    ## The Key Discovery
    
    An integer n ≥ 2 is prime if and only if (3n³ − 13n² + 18n − 8)M₁(n) + (12n² − 120n + 212)M₂(n) − 960M₃(n) = 0, where the M_a(n) are MacMahon's well-studied partition functions.
    
    More generally, for MacMahonesque partition functions, there are infinitely many such prime detecting equations with constant coefficients.
    
    ## Recent Developments
    
    This work was recognized as the runner-up for the 2025 Cozzarelli Prize, which recognizes the best paper of the year in the Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences.
    
    More recent follow-up work has expanded on these results. Building on the results of Craig, van Ittersum, and Ono, researchers provide a refined understanding of MacMahon's partition functions and their variants, including their quasi-modular properties and new prime-detecting expressions.
    
    This research represents a surprising connection between additive number theory (integer partitions) and multiplicative number theory (prime detection).
    
    
