    Based on my search results, here's information about sieve algorithms and prime counting complexity:
    
    ## Sieve Algorithm Complexity
    
    The Sieve of Eratosthenes has a time complexity of O(n log log n), making it one of the most efficient ways to find all primes up to a given limit.
    
    For more specialized variants:
    
    - **Segmented Sieve**: The time complexity of the segmented sieve approach is O((R - L + 1) log log (R) + √R log log √R).
    
    - **Page Segmented Version**: The normally implemented page segmented version has the same operational complexity of O(n log log n) as the non-segmented version but reduces the space requirements to the very minimal size of the segment page plus the memory required to store the base primes less than the square root of the range.
    
    ## Prime Counting Function Algorithms
    
    For computing the nth prime, more sophisticated approaches are discussed in recent research. Using existing results on the complexity of evaluating the prime-counting function π(x), a binary search approach computes p_n in O(√n·(log n)⁴) time. 
    
    Assuming the Riemann Hypothesis and Cramér's conjecture, an improved sieve-based algorithm runs in O(√n·(log^(7/2) n)·log log n) time, though this improvement is conditional.
    
    The search results also include an arxiv paper (2604.02383) on "Neural Prime Sieves" that uses machine learning approaches to predict prime family membership, though it focuses on a different problem than traditional sieve complexity analysis.
    
    
