    Based on the search results, I found relevant information about algorithms for computing the nth prime and their complexity. Here are the key findings:
    
    ## Binary Search Approach
    
    Using existing results on the complexity of evaluating the prime-counting function π(x), the binary search approach computes pₙ in O(√n(log n)⁴) time.
    
    ## Conditional Improvements
    
    Assuming the Riemann Hypothesis and Cramér's conjecture, a tighter interval around the approximation can be constructed that achieves O(√n(log^(7/2)n)log log n) time.
    
    ## Sieve Comparisons
    
    The fastest sieve, which is a sublinear sieve, cannot beat the binary search algorithm asymptotically. With current state of bounds, a segmented sieve over an interval cannot beat the binary search algorithm without further work.
    
    ## Practical Implementation
    
    The most efficient known method for calculating the nth prime is a combination of the prime counting function and a prime sieve, using a close approximation of the nth prime using the inverse logarithmic integral or the inverse Riemann R function, then counting the primes up to this guess using the prime counting function, and finally sieving using the segmented sieve of Eratosthenes until finding the actual nth prime.
    
    
