    6	## Why these analogies?
    7	
    8	| Breakthrough | Mechanism | Analog for π(x)? |
    9	|---|---|---|
    10	| Shor (factoring) | QFT exposes period | Spectral structure in oscillatory zero sum |
    11	| Compressed sensing | Sparsity in transform basis | Wavelet/Padé sparsity of residual |
    12	| AlphaFold | Learned energy landscape | NN learns δ(n) = p(n) − R⁻¹(n)? |
    13	| Fast multipole | Hierarchical truncation of long-range kernel | The zero sum IS a long-range kernel — multipole moments? |
    14	| Borel resummation | Divergent → fast-converging | Explicit formula is conditionally convergent |
    15	
    16	## Selected experiments (each gets a script)
    17	
    18	### 1. Borel resummation of the explicit-formula zero sum (`borel_explicit_formula.py`)
    19	The truncated zero sum Σ_{|γ|<T} x^ρ/ρ converges only as T → ∞ at rate ~1/log T. Apply
    20	Borel transform to the *coefficient sequence in T* and integrate. If the Borel
    21	transform analytically continues to a tractable function on ℝ⁺, we collapse infinite
    22	zeros into one integral evaluation. Free lunch test: compute partial sums S_K(x),
    23	form Σ_k S_k z^k / k!, integrate against e^{−z}. Does it converge faster than S_K?
    24	
    25	### 2. Padé approximants of the residual δ(n) = p(n) − R⁻¹(n) (`pade_residual.py`)
    26	δ(n) is "random-like" in 21 measures, but Padé approximants exploit pole structure not
    27	captured by Fourier/wavelet. If δ(n) has hidden meromorphic structure when interpolated
    28	as a continuous function, even a low-order Padé should give super-Gaussian compression.
    29	Compare RMSE of degree-(M, N) Padé vs polynomial of degree M+N.
