    ## Current Algorithm Complexity
    
    The most efficient practical algorithms for computing π(x) have the following complexities:
    
    The Lagarias-Miller-Odlyzko algorithm (published in 1985) has a lower runtime complexity of O(x^(2/3)/log x) operations.
    
    Improvements refined the Lagarias-Miller-Odlyzko method allowing one to save a factor of log x in the time complexity, and Gourdon published refinements to the Deléglise-Rivat method that saves constant factors in time and space complexity.
    
    ## Classical Algorithms
    
    For context, earlier algorithms had higher complexities:
    
    - The sieve of Eratosthenes has a running time of O(x log log x) operations
    - Legendre's formula has a running time of O(∛x)
    
    ## Recent Work
    
    A 2020 manuscript proposed a method to compute π(x) with time complexity O(x^(1/2)) without the need to introduce the non-trivial zeros of the Riemann zeta function, though this appears to be theoretical work.
    
