    Based on the search results, here's what I found about the Guth-Maynard work on zeta zeros density theorem and prime counting:
    
    ## The 2024 Breakthrough
    
    Guth and Maynard proved new bounds for how often Dirichlet polynomials can take large values, giving improved estimates for a Dirichlet polynomial of length N taking values of size close to N^(3/4), which is the critical situation for several estimates in analytic number theory connected to prime numbers and the Riemann zeta function.
    
    ## Zero Density Estimates
    
    As a consequence, they deduced a zero density estimate N(σ, T) ≤ T^(30(1−σ)/13+o(1)) and asymptotics for primes in short intervals of length x^(17/30+o(1)).
    
    More specifically, Guth and Maynard managed to improve the Ingham bound from 3/5=0.6 to 13/25=0.52. This represents the first substantial improvement to a bound that had stood for 80 years.
    
    ## Prime Counting Consequences
    
    The range for which we can prove a prime number theorem in almost all short intervals now improves from θ>1/6=0.166... to θ>2/15=0.133...
    
    The 2024 breakthrough by Guth and Maynard significantly improved estimates related to the distribution of primes in short intervals, a problem closely linked to the Riemann Hypothesis. Their work, building on Ingham's estimates, brings mathematicians closer to the error bounds predicted by RH for the prime number theorem in short intervals.
    
    
